  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 PM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nThis Afternoon - Friday: Cooler, Periodic Sprinkles/Flurries\n\nCold air advection is steadily building southward as winds \nswitch from E to NNW in the wake of a compact area of low \npressure that is tracking through IL/IN early this afternoon. \nLower tropospheric temperatures fall from +3C to -7C by \nThursday morning as polar air spreads southward from northern \nMN/southern Ontario. Highs for tomorrow and Friday will struggle\nto reach the low 40s as continued cold air advection takes \nplace under broad cyclonic upper tropospheric flow. \n\nA series of undulations/waves ripple through the region between\nthis afternoon and Friday morning. While moisture is limited \nabove 800-mb, steep low-level lapse rates in conjunction with a \nfetch of moisture off Lake Superior look to be enough to support\nat least periodic sprinkles or even flurries (depending on the\ndepth of the boundary layer moisture, which is struggling to \nreach -10C at times) with each of these waves. Little to no \nimpacts are expected from these very light showers with the \nregions impacted gradually shifting eastward over the ensuing \n24-36 hours as the upper level pattern ever so slowly migrates \neastward. The exact timing of these waves is tough to nail down \nfrom an hourly forecast perspective, so the mention of \nsprinkles/flurries was kept broad at this time.\n\nThe Weekend into Early Next Week: Dry and Slowly Warming\n\nAn Omega Block sets up over the High Plains for Friday into the\nweekend and redevelops early next week as a stronger subsequent\nridge merges in from the west. The aforementioned meridional\n1023-mb surface high pressure cell gradually slides from MN-MO \nto the lower Great Lakes/upper Ohio River Valley and deepens to\naround 1035-mb between Friday and Tuesday, resulting in a more \nmoderating flow pattern becoming established through the weekend\ninto early next week. The medium range ensembles are \ngravitating towards next Thursday-Saturday being the timeframe \nof seeing the warming temperatures from this pattern shift, but \neven these only look to reach into the mid to upper 50s. Little\nif any precipitation is forecast with these ridges in firm\ncontrol.\n\nThis amplification of the upper level pattern for the end of \nnext week does set the stage for a potentially more active \nstretch of weather leading into the Thanksgiving holiday. How \nthis pattern evolves remains to be seen, but impactful snow\nsomewhere in the Upper Midwest is one plausible scenario that \nis showing up in a few ensemble members.",
